                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h2]𝐃𝖔𝖓'𝖙 𝖈𝖆𝖑𝖑 𝖒𝖊 𝐂𝖍𝖆𝖗𝖑𝖎𝖊[/h2]\n\nThis is the most disturbing game I've played in a while.\nSome of the stories you have to investigate and the things you witness are despicable…\nIn many cases, the choices you're presented with are, how should I put it:\n[b][Suicide][/b] or [b][Give up][/b]\n\nThe whole time I played, there was this creeping feeling of inevitability — nothing matters, and I knew it would only get worse. \nThen the ending came… and somehow made it all even worse.\nSo no, you won't find a happy ending here...\n\n[h1] ıllıllı 𝖲𝗍𝗋𝗈𝗇𝗀 𝖲𝗂𝖽𝖾𝗌 𝗈𝖿 𝗍𝗁𝖾 𝖦𝖺𝗆𝖾 ıllıllı [/h1]\n[list]\n[*] ☑️ Believable character writing — their emotions and actions feel incredibly real\n[*] ☑️ Great VA work, from the main character down to several side characters as well\n[*] ☑️ Dark, oppressive atmosphere that never lets you go\n[*] ☑️ [b]«Mind Palace»[/b] and [b]«Retrocognition»[/b] mechanics make you feel like a true detective\n[/list]\n\n\n[h1] ıllıllı 𝖶𝖾𝖺𝗄𝗇𝖾𝗌𝗌𝖾𝗌 𝗈𝖿 𝗍𝗁𝖾 𝖦𝖺𝗆𝖾 ıllıllı [/h1]\n[list]\n[*] 🔸 Many identical NPC faces recycled over and over\n[*] 🔸 Weak and clunky combat system\n[*] 🔸 Heavy traversal stutters and broken DLSS FrameGen \n[i](remove [b]amd_fidelityfx_dx12.dll [/b] from game folder to fix FG) [/i]\n[/list]",
